--     `facts.itemKey`): "known" means **every character** in the priority list
--     knows the item; "not-known" means at least one doesn't. `canUnlock` is the
--     mirror: "can-unlock" is "not all know it" (opening would teach someone);
--     "cannot-unlock" is "all know it" (no one would learn).
--   - For other items (collectibles — outfit style pages, trophy/runebox
--     fragments): there is no per-character `itemKey`. Their unlock-state is the
--     account-wide `facts.known` bit (addon `IsCollectibleUnlocked` /
--     `IsCollectibleOwnedByDefId`, mirrored to web/CLI via the synced snapshot).
--     When `itemKey` is absent but `facts.known` is set, the same known/canUnlock
--     decision is taken from that single account-wide bit (an account-wide unlock
--     is treated as "all characters know it").
--   - When BOTH `itemKey` and `known` are absent, the producer's authoritative
--     `facts.isKnowledgeItem` bit decides: `false` (item has no knowledge
--     semantics — e.g. a generic non-collectible container) → `fail`, so the
--     rule cleanly doesn't apply and the walk continues deterministically
--     instead of stranding on an indeterminate. `true` (a knowledge item whose
--     unlock state could not be resolved) or `undefined` (legacy producer) →
--     `indeterminate` with `missingSignal: "itemKey"`, preserving the ItemFacts
--     "`undefined` = unknown" contract.
